# The realm's common speech (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the bottom layer of every speaker's grammar, and
# Ridgemere's own. Every symbol a rule or a dialect uses must be here, so any dialect can leave a word out and fall
# back to ordinary speech.
#
#   symbol = words | words              one of them, at random
#   symbol[facts] = words               only where the facts hold; the most specific entry that holds wins
#   symbol! = words | words             a habit: chosen once per person, for good
#   symbol!! = words | words            a habit for each one they speak to (a fond name for each friend)
#   symbol.broad = words                (in a dialect) said only by the broadest accents
#   words^m | words^f                   for one sex of the one spoken to
#
# Each symbol is one kind of word, so it fits wherever it is used: cold, hot, tired, drunk, good, bad, very are
# words like "cold" ("it's #very# #cold#"); rain, money, luck, nonsense, child, home are things ("this #rain#"); greet,
# farewell, thanks, agree, refuse, surprise, annoyed, oath are whole sayings; tag ends a sentence (", eh").
#
# A speaker's dialect (the grammar named for where they grew up) is used as their accent says: none, never; light,
# half the time; broad, always, with its .broad words. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar plain
greet = Good day | Hello | Morning
greet[here.time=dawn] = Morning | Good morning
greet[here.time=dusk|night] = Evening | Good evening
farewell = Farewell | Good day to you | Mind how you go
thanks = Thank you | Thanks | Much obliged
agree = Aye | True enough | That it is
refuse = No | Not likely | I'd rather not
surprise = Well! | Is that so | You don't say
annoyed = Bother | Curse it | Of all the luck
very = very | right | terribly
little = a little | a bit | a touch
good = fine | good | sound
bad = poor | bad | sorry
worthless = worthless | no use | rubbish
luck = luck | good fortune | a stroke of luck
child = child | pup
address = friend | neighbour
address[other.stage=young] = little one | lad^m | lass^f
pet!! = my dear | love | old friend
money = coin | money | pennies
cold = cold | bitter | raw
hot = hot | close | stifling
rain = rain | wet | downpour
tired = tired | worn out | weary
drunk = drunk | merry | tipsy
nonsense = nonsense | rubbish | foolishness
tag = , isn't it | , eh | , wouldn't you say
oath! = Gods | Heavens | Blood and bread | Well, I never
robbery = Robbery, plain and simple | Daylight robbery | A crime, that is
home = home | my own fire
hungry = hungry | starved | empty as a drum
hurt = sore | stiff | aching
work = work | the work | graft
bed = bed | my bed | a pillow
# For each person's own words (their character table, doc 62 phase 6): a saying of their own, and what they call
# their spouse. Plain's are the ordinary ones; a person's own layer gives theirs.
saying! = Well, there it is | That's how it goes | So it goes | Such is life
dear! = love | dear | my heart

# The router's openers (phase 5): what a resident says before an answer, by tone. ~ is nothing at all.
opener = ~ | ~ | Well,
opener[self.tone=warm] = Of course! | Happy to help. | Ah, let me think.
opener[self.tone=gruff] = ~ | Hmph. | Listen.
opener[self.tone=formal] = Certainly. | Of course. | Allow me.
opener[self.tone=sly] = For you? | Ah, well now. | Since you ask,
opener[self.tone=shy] = Oh, um. | I, well. | Oh!

# Phase 7's words (the conversations written for the main factors and the everyday topics). Each is one kind of word:
# things take their article where shown ("#watch# took them", "in #gaol#", "after #supper#"); a person without one
# ("a #thief#", "a #stranger#"); words like "busy" or "poorly" fit where "cold" does ("you look #poorly#"); whole
# sayings stand alone (#toast#, #blessing#, #bless#).
# Work and trade.
wages = wages | pay | pennies
boss = the master | the boss | the old wolf
busy = busy | rushed | run ragged
slow = slow | quiet | dead
takings = takings | the till | the day's money
cheated = cheated | robbed blind | done
# Family: what one calls their mother and father (habits: always the same), grandparents, the evening meal.
mum! = Mum | Mam | Mother | Ma
dad! = Dad | Da | Father | Pa
gran = Gran | Granny | Nan | Grandmother
grandad = Grandad | Gramps | Grandfather | Grandda
supper = supper | dinner | tea
# Town and class: the rich as the rest see them, the poor as the rich see them, out of money, grand, a tattler, a
# polite word to one's betters.
toffs = the toffs | the nobs | the quality | the fine folk
rabble = the rabble | the riff-raff | that lot | the gutter crowd
skint = skint | broke | cleaned out | short
posh = posh | grand | fancy | la-di-da
busybody = busybody | nosy parker | gossip | tattler
sir = sir^m | madam^f
# Friends and rivals: a rival to their face, a friend teased, mad, a toast, a drink to stand someone, a fool.
insult = you mangy cur | you lout | you sour old stick | you flea-bitten so-and-so
fool = you daft lump | you great fool | you muttonhead | you soft article
daft = daft | soft in the head | cracked | barmy
toast = Your health | Here's to us | Fur and fortune | Down the hatch
drink = a jar | a drink | a pint | a cup of cider | a drop of something
lump = great lump | oaf | lummox | daft article
later = later | by and by | another time
# A talk turning (doc 84 §8.5, Phase 5): a step to a neighbouring topic, and a marked change of subject.
pivot = Speaking of which. | That puts me in mind of something. | While we're at it. | Which reminds me. | And another thing.
# How a dispreferred answer softens (doc 84 §7.2, Phase 6: a temperament weighs a hedged line; Phase 7 gives each dialect its own).
hedge = Well | I don't know | Begging your pardon | Now then | Forgive me
anyway = Anyway. | But never mind that. | Still, enough of that. | Something else, while I think of it. | Here's a thing.
# The moment.
fog = fog | mist | murk
snow = snow | white stuff
mud = mud | muck | mire
wind = wind | gale | blow
ale = ale | beer | brown ale
# Hard times: the law and the want.
watch = the Watch | the watch
thief = thief | cutpurse | light-pawed sort
bandits = bandits | robbers | road wolves
gaol = the gaol | the cells | the lock-up
dole = the dole | the parish bread | the parish loaf
grave = the ground | the grave | the churchyard
# Food and the house.
grub = food | grub | victuals
tasty = tasty | toothsome | lovely
mess = mess | state | tip
snug = snug | cosy | warm
# Pastimes and beasts.
beasts = the beasts | the stock | the animals
bet = a wager | a bet | a flutter
# Health and the past.
poorly = poorly | under the weather | off colour | out of sorts
chill = a chill | a cold | the sniffles
remedy = willow bark | a mustard poultice | goose grease | honey and onion | a nettle brew | a hot brick in a cloth
olddays = the old days | days gone by | the good old days
# Blessings, and someday.
blessing = The Bright One keep you | Bless you | Blessings on you | Bright One watch over you
bless = Gods keep you | Bright One bless you | The gods go with you
someday = someday | one day | one fine day
# Travel.
far = a long way | a fair step | miles off
stranger = stranger | outsider | newcomer
journey = journey | trek | long haul
# The dialogue window's words (Docs/Design/83-dialogue-trees.md): a keeper at the counter, and work offered.
shop_offer = What'll it be? | What can I get you? | Have a look. | See anything you like?
shop_dear = Prices are what they are. | Nothing's cheap this week. | Dear as ever, I'm afraid.
shop_sold = There you are. | Good choice. | Pleasure doing business. | Mind you don't drop it.
shop_bye = Come again. | Come back soon. | Mind how you go.
shop_bare = My shelves are bare just now. | Not much left, I'm afraid. | Cleared out, near enough.
shop_asleep = Mm... shut. Come back in the morning. | Zzz... later... the shop's shut. | Mm? Not now. Morning.
talk_open = What can I do for you? | Something on your mind? | What is it?
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2): work offered, taken, paid, given back.
work_offer = There's work, if you want it. | I could use a hand. | Something needs doing.
work_taken = Good. Don't dawdle. | Good. I'll hold you to it. | That's settled, then.
work_paid = Here, as promised. | Paid, and well earned. | There you are. Fairly done.
work_back = No harm done. I'll find another. | Fair enough. Someone else will take it. | Better said now than never.
work_none = Nothing going just now. | No work today, I'm afraid. | Nothing that needs doing.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3): an inn's welcome and its beds, the watch, the church, a master's trade.
inn_offer = Bed, board or a drink? | Come in out of the weather. | What'll it be: a bed or a bite?
inn_bed = Upstairs, and mind the step. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's made upstairs. Sleep well. | Up the stairs. There's a chest by the bed.
watch_greet = Move along, unless you've business with the watch. | The watch. What is it? | Keep the peace and we'll get on.
church_greet = Peace be on you. | Welcome, all are welcome here. | Come in. Rest a while.
learn_offer = It's a trade worth knowing. | It's honest work, and steady. | It keeps a roof over us.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over.
news_none = Quiet enough, these days. | Nothing worth the telling. | Not that I've heard.
gossip_none = I don't hold with gossip. | Nothing I'd repeat. | Not that I've heard.
trouble_none = Oh, nothing worth your time. | Nothing, thank you.
trouble_thanks = Thank you. I won't forget it. | You're kind. Thank you. | I don't know what to say. Thank you.
way_ask = Where are you headed? | Where to? | Looking for somewhere?
job_busy = I keep busy. | This and that. | Work, mostly.
work_declined = Suit yourself. | Another time, then. | As you like.
inn_rest = Sit by the fire, then. A bed upstairs rests you properly, mind. | Take the bench by the fire. Nobody will move you.
watch_noted = Noted. We'll see to it. | Noted. The watch thanks you. | Good. We'll find them.
watch_bye = Move along, then. | Keep the peace.
learn_taken = Good. Be here when I start, and stay near me while I work. | Then you're mine to teach. Stay close while I work.
receiver_ask = Something for me? | Yes? Is that for me?
inn_helper = I only help here. #here.innkeeper# keeps the house, and the rooms are theirs to let. | Ask #here.innkeeper# for a bed. I just carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): the turns every conversation has, each a kind of word (§7.2): how a refusal
# softens and what it gives as its reason, taking in what was said, closing, an excuse to go, news one had, sympathy,
# giving ground and holding it, a warning, a second meeting, a welcome, an apology and its acceptance, thanks waved
# off, a refusal taken well, yes to "are you busy?", not now, a worry led in, reassurance, joy at good news, a view
# not shared, news not believed, and calling the ear. Said by rules/discourse.talk's move lines and the vent lines;
# written for Phase 8's. Drafts; the dialects' to be tuned by ear (§10.3).
# the reason a dispreferred answer gives (§7.2: hedge, account, remedy)
account = I've too much on just now | My hands are full | It's not a good time | I've my own to see to
# a turn that takes in what was said and owes nothing (ack)
ack = I see | Is that so | Well, well | Fancy that | There it is
# taking up a talk that was broken off (return, §8.10)
resume = As I was saying | Where was I, now | Now, where was I | As I was telling you
# the first step of a closing (§8.4): the talk is near its end
preclose = Well. There it is | Still. Can't stand about | So. There we are | Well, that's that
# why one goes (a preclose's reason)
excuse = I've things to see to | I'm wanted at home | Work won't wait
# news one had already (react.news.known)
knew = I'd heard | So I heard | That's old news
# feeling for one's trouble (sympathise)
sympathy = I'm sorry for it | That's a weight to carry | It's a heavy load
# giving ground (concede)
concede = Maybe so | You may be right | I'll grant you that
# holding one's ground (insist)
insist = I'll say it again | Mark my words | I know what I know
# a warning's words (warn)
warn = Mind yourself | Watch your step | Have a care
# a second meeting the same day (again.open): said as a question
welcome_back = Back again | You again | Twice in a day
# one who joins, welcomed (welcome_join)
welcome = Come and join us | Pull up, then | All the better
# an apology (apologise)
sorry = I meant nothing by it | No offence meant | Forgive me
# an apology taken (accept_apology)
forgive = No harm done | Think nothing of it | It's forgotten
# thanks waved off (deflect_thanks)
deflect = Any time | Don't mention it | It's nothing
# a refusal taken well (accept_decline)
no_matter = Another time, then | No matter | Fair enough
# yes to "are you busy?" (go_ahead)
go_ahead = Ask away | Go on, then | Out with it
# not now (put_off)
put_off = Not just now | Another time, maybe
# how a worry is led in, to a friend (the vent goal, §7.4)
vent = Truth be told | Between us | If I'm honest | I'll tell you straight
# it will pass (reassure)
reassure = It'll come right | Don't fret | You'll manage
# joy at one's good news (congratulate)
congrats = Well done | Good for you | Well done, you
# a view not shared, softly (disagree)
demur = I don't see it that way | I'm not so sure | I'd not go that far
# news not believed yet (react.news.doubt)
doubt = I'm not so sure | I'd want more than that | I'll believe it when I see it
# calling the other's ear first
listen = Listen | Here | Mark this

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7; tools/talk_migrate.py): the library's most repeated short answers, each a
# symbol whose value here is exactly the words it replaced, so plain speech is unchanged (talk_migrate.py --check);
# each dialect says them its own way. A light accent uses the dialect's half the time, a broad one always.
bc_good = Good.
bc_always = Always.
bc_thank_you = Thank you.
bc_it_is = It is.
bc_ha = Ha.
bc_i_will = I will.
bc_very = Very.
bc_never = Never.
bc_probably = Probably.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see.
bc_maybe = Maybe.
bc_me_too = Me too.
bc_it_does = It does.
bc_again = Again.
bc_lovely = Lovely.
bc_every_year = Every year.
bc_wise = Wise.
bc_i_know = I know.
bc_so_they_say = So they say.
bc_again_q = Again?
bc_of_course = Of course.
bc_about_time = About time.
bc_mine_too = Mine too.
bc_ill_try = I'll try.
bc_nor_me = Nor me.
bc_they_do = They do.
bc_poor_soul = Poor soul.
bc_i_wont = I won't.
bc_every_one = Every one.
bc_not_me = Not me.
bc_very_good = Very good.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then.
bc_barely = Barely.
bc_lucky_you = Lucky you.
bc_every_night = Every night.
bc_nobody = Nobody.
bc_for_once = For once.
bc_i_hope_so = I hope so.
bc_exactly = Exactly.
bc_everything = Everything.
bc_true = True.
bc_mostly = Mostly.
bc_gladly = Gladly.
bc_fair_enough = Fair enough.
bc_sorry = Sorry.
bc_every_time = Every time.
bc_very_bad = Very bad.
bc_fair = Fair.
bc_bless_you = Bless you.
bc_always_is = Always is.
bc_right = Right.
bc_lucky = Lucky.
bc_bad_luck = Bad luck.
bc_why_q = Why?
bc_true_enough = True enough.
bc_they_always_do = They always do.
bc_every_day = Every day.
bc_very_busy = Very busy.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry.
bc_so_do_i = So do I.
bc_clever = Clever.
bc_too_late = Too late.
bc_go_on = Go on.
bc_we_will = We will.
bc_they_are = They are.
bc_so_am_i = So am I.
bc_let_them = Let them.
bc_hope_so = Hope so.
bc_already = Already.
bc_i_might = I might.
bc_the_best = The best.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to.
bc_nothing = Nothing.
bc_and_q = And?
bc_who_q = Who?
bc_it_was = It was.
